---

<Doc
  title="Getting started"
  description="Install mxwl, configure a project, and open your first workspace."
>
  <p>
    mxwl brings a browser, code editor, Git review, terminals, and coding agents into a workspace
    anchored to a folder. You can work locally or connect to a machine over SSH.
  </p>
  <h2>1. Install the editor</h2>
  <p>On Linux x86_64, run the installer:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={product.installer} label="Linux install" />
  <p>
    The installer verifies the AppImage checksum, installs <code>mxwl</code> in{' '}
    <code>~/.local/bin</code>, and adds a desktop launcher. Re-run it to update. You need{' '}
    <code>curl</code> and <code>sha256sum</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a Linux .deb or a macOS zip, use the <a href="/download/">download page</a>. mxwl is an
    early alpha; read the <a href="/docs/alpha/">current limitations</a> before relying on it for
    daily work.
  </p>
  <h2>2. Create a project</h2>
  <p>
    Open <strong>Projects</strong> and create a project. A project holds shared settings for browser
    profiles, service commands, file exclusions, integrations, AI tasks, and plugin visibility. You
    can create it before you have a checkout.
  </p>
  <h2>3. Configure a host</h2>
  <p>
    Every project has a permanent <strong>This machine</strong> host. Choose{' '}
    <strong>Configure checkout</strong> to set its checkout and workspaces paths. To attach a remote
    machine, choose <strong>Add host</strong> and select an SSH or Tailscale connection.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each host can have different paths and settings. The same project can live on several machines.
    See <a href="/docs/projects/">Projects, hosts & workspaces</a> for the full setup.
  </p>
  <h2>4. Open your workspace</h2>
  <p>
    Select the host and choose <strong>Open workspaces</strong>. Each folder becomes a tab with its
    own browser, code, changes, agents, terminals, and layout.
  </p>
  <p>
    Use <strong>Projects → project → host</strong> to move around. Workspaces on other hosts keep
    running while you switch.
  </p>
  <h2>5. Bring an agent</h2>
  <p>
    Open the <strong>Agent</strong> tab and select your provider. mxwl uses the Agent Client
    Protocol. Install or authenticate the provider’s tooling on the machine where the workspace
    lives. Your agent’s provider billing and authentication are separate from mxwl.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start with a small task, inspect the tool calls, then open <strong>Changes</strong> to review
    the result. Learn more in the <a href="/docs/agents/">agent runtime guide</a>.
  </p>
  <h2 id="a-few-useful-shortcuts">A few useful shortcuts</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Shortcut</th>
        <th>Action</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Ctrl/⌘ K</td>
        <td>Search files, workspaces, agents, and commands</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ctrl/⌘ P</td>
        <td>Quick-open a file</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ctrl/⌘ T</td>
        <td>Open a workspace</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ctrl/⌘ Shift T</td>
        <td>Launch a ticket worktree, sandbox, and agent</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ctrl/⌘ Shift 1 / 2 / 3</td>
        <td>Maximize browser, code, or bottom pane</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Esc</td>
        <td>Restore a pane or close a dialog</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="/tour/">Take the tour</a> to see how the pieces fit together.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/browser/">Create cookie sandboxes</a> for different test identities.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/agent-control/">Set up fleet notifications, the CLI, or mobile control.</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/docs/plugins/">Add your own workspace tool.</a>
    </li>
  </ul>
</Doc>
